     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is double the usual maximum of about 2,550 mg per day for adults, and most guidelines cap it at 2,000\u20132,550 mg. Doses this high raise the risk of severe GI effects and lactic acidosis. A single 1,700 mg dose is also well above normal. It may be a typo, for example 1,700 mg total per day, or 850 mg twice daily.\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, who can confirm the indication, dose, and patient details.\n\nPlease have the prescriber confirm the intended dose. Once I have a clinically valid dose and confirmation from the prescriber, I can enter it. A typical regimen would be 500 mg or 850 mg by mouth two or three times daily with meals, staying under the daily maximum."
      }
     ],
